{:edb/db-before-t 1617 :edb/db-after-t 1618 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534930 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:44:14.244Z" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186118071 1078 #edb/ref 17592186118075 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186119102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186119103 13194139534930 17592186045417 false] [17592186119102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186119105 13194139534930 17592186045417 false] [17592186119102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186119106 13194139534930 17592186045417 false] [17592186119102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186119107 13194139534930 17592186045417 false] [17592186119102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444662 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186119102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444664 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186119102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444681 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186119102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444684 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186119102 1078 #edb/ref 17592186444681 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186119758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444696 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186119759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444693 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186119759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444694 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186119760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444695 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186119760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444697 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186120034 1050 1080.0 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186120322 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186120322 1071 "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$. Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n$T(s)|=-\\frac{4}{5}\\sin (\\frac{s}{2})i + \\frac{3}{5}\\sin (\\frac{s}{2})j + \\cos (\\frac{s}{2})k$." 13194139534930 17592186045417 false] [17592186120322 1071 "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186120322 1074 "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))k \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,k] \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))^{2}+(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})+9{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})+25{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{25({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,}=2.\n$$" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186120498 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186120498 1071 "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0, 1)$ where $t = 0$, given that $‖r^{′}(0)‖ = 1$, and the unit tangent vector to the curve is\n$T(t) = ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + t^{2}}}, \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1 + t^{2}}}⟩$." 13194139534930 17592186045417 false] [17592186120498 1071 "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,1)$ where $t=0$, given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=1,$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟩\\,.\n$$" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186120498 1074 "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨0,1⟩,$ since the point $P(0,1)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖$: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{({0}^{2}+1)^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({0}^{2}+1)^{3}}}⟩=⟨0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{1}=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨0,1⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨0,1⟩+1⟨0,1⟩=⟨0,2⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186121010 1064 "If a curve has a curvature of zero at some point, the radius of curvature at that point is defined to be infinite.\n\nIt follows from the definition that a straight line has zero curvature at all of its points. Conversely, if a curve has zero curvature at any point, it must be contained on a straight line.\n\nSuppose now that we want to find the radius of curvature for the curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t^2, \\: 1 - t^2, \\: 1+ t^2 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nat an arbitrary point where $t> 0.$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and its magnitude $\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,-2t,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we used the fact that $\\sqrt{t^2}=t$ since $t>0.$\n\nNext, we find the unit tangent and its derivative for $t>0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{'}(t)}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}t}\\cdot ⟨2t,\\,-2t,\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\ {T}^{'}(t) & =⟨0,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{'}(t)‖}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}t}{0}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, despite its \"parabolic\" appearance, the given curve is contained on a straight line!\n\n**Note:** Often, the unit tangent vector of a curve $\\mathbf T(t)$ may look a bit complicated. In such cases, the following formula involving a cross product may be useful. It says that the radius of curvature of a twice-differentiable curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nR(t)=  \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(t)} =\\frac{{{{\\left\\| {\\mathbf r'\\left( t \\right)} \\right\\|}^3}}}{{\\left\\| {\\mathbf r'\\left( t \\right) \\times \\mathbf r''\\left( t \\right)} \\right\\|}}.\n$$" 13194139534930 17592186045417 false] [17592186121010 1064 "If a curve has a curvature of zero at some point, the radius of curvature at that point is defined to be infinite.\n\nIt follows from the definition that a straight line has zero curvature at all of its points. Conversely, if a curve has zero curvature at any point, it must be contained on a straight line.\n\nSuppose now that we want to find the radius of curvature for the curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t^2, \\: 1 - t^2, \\: 1+ t^2 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nat an arbitrary point where $t> 0.$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and its magnitude $\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,-2t,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we used the fact that $\\sqrt{t^2}=t$ since $t>0.$\n\nNext, we find the unit tangent and its derivative for $t>0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}t}\\cdot ⟨2t,\\,-2t,\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(t) & =⟨0,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}t}{0}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, despite its \"parabolic\" appearance, the given curve is contained on a straight line!\n\n**Note:** Often, the unit tangent vector of a curve $\\mathbf T(t)$ may look a bit complicated. In such cases, the following formula involving a cross product may be useful. It says that the radius of curvature of a twice-differentiable curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nR(t)=  \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(t)} =\\frac{{{{\\left\\| {\\mathbf r'\\left( t \\right)} \\right\\|}^3}}}{{\\left\\| {\\mathbf r'\\left( t \\right) \\times \\mathbf r''\\left( t \\right)} \\right\\|}}.\n$$" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444659 1080 "The center is $(2,0)$ and the radius $1$" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444659 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444659 1083 #uuid "0e7c676a-8ada-5b80-8687-e725bb845066" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444660 1080 "The center is $(3,1)$ and the radius $2$" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444660 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444660 1083 #uuid "160ecfc6-2535-55df-b403-21e3404605cd" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444661 1080 "\\frac{27}{8}" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444661 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444661 1083 #uuid "17dfe97c-105a-5dbd-a5d8-f67bff74a3eb" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444662 1080 "The center is $(1,0)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444662 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444662 1083 #uuid "18864349-f844-598b-8bd8-2499081b262c" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444663 1080 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444663 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444663 1083 #uuid "2224ac84-3f36-51f0-b57f-7543c32e2f98" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444664 1080 "The center is $(1,1)$ and the radius is $2$" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444664 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444664 1083 #uuid "26713c2f-51c0-5113-aace-e97dae2a9d84" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444665 1080 "The center is $⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{5}{3}⟩$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444665 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534930 17592186045417 true] [17592186444665 1083 #uuid "3